WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.336 --> 00:00:05.221
- If you're good at what you
do, people will recognize that.

00:00:05.221 --> 00:00:07.166
From grade one to six
you're in this one school.

00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:08.404
And then you go to this new school.

00:00:08.404 --> 00:00:11.936
I went to this new school,
and there was this girl.

00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:13.490
This girl named Sandy.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:16.607
I stopped listening to
music after Kurt died

00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:18.372
because it was so
emotional just hearing it.

00:00:18.372 --> 00:00:20.585
I love being a drummer.

00:00:20.585 --> 00:00:24.151
It's such a nice position in a band.

00:00:24.151 --> 00:00:25.693
Everyone thinks you're dumb.

00:00:25.693 --> 00:00:28.985
I can truthfully say
aloud that Gangnam Style

00:00:28.985 --> 00:00:32.318
is one of my favorite
songs of the past decade.

00:00:32.318 --> 00:00:34.262
- He's an American rock musician,

00:00:34.262 --> 00:00:37.235
singer, songwriter, director and producer.

00:00:37.235 --> 00:00:38.988
He's best known as the former drummer

00:00:38.988 --> 00:00:40.310
of the grunge band Nirvana,

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.950
and founder and front man of
the rock band Foo Fighters.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.794
In 2012, he was estimated to be

00:00:45.794 --> 00:00:48.141
the third wealthiest drummer in the world

00:00:48.141 --> 00:00:51.203
with a fortune of $225 million.

00:00:51.203 --> 00:00:53.630
He's Dave Grohl, and here's my take

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.606
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:55.606 --> 00:00:57.707
Rule number eight is my personal favorite,

00:00:57.707 --> 00:00:58.856
and I'm curious to figure out

00:00:58.856 --> 00:01:00.086
which one you guys like the best,

00:01:00.086 --> 00:01:01.519
As you're watching Dave,

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:02.949
if he says something
really meaningful to you,

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:05.417
make sure to put it in
quotes in the comments below

00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:08.750
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:01:11.822 --> 00:01:16.665
- If you're good at what you
do, people will recognize that.

00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:18.613
I really believe it.

00:01:18.613 --> 00:01:19.909
I really believe that going out

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:22.159
and playing good songs live

00:01:23.127 --> 00:01:25.062
as a great live band

00:01:25.062 --> 00:01:27.145
will make you successful.

00:01:28.616 --> 00:01:29.944
I really think it will.

00:01:29.944 --> 00:01:31.543
And it doesn't matter if you're at the--

00:01:31.543 --> 00:01:34.453
down the street or you're on
the side stage of Bonnaroo

00:01:34.453 --> 00:01:37.474
or you're headlining Lalapalooza.

00:01:37.474 --> 00:01:40.068
If you're a great band with great songs,

00:01:40.068 --> 00:01:42.204
people will notice it.

00:01:42.204 --> 00:01:44.672
That's it, that's all it is.

00:01:44.672 --> 00:01:46.371
It's that simple.

00:01:46.371 --> 00:01:50.204
- - product placement
and labels and A&amp;R people

00:01:53.476 --> 00:01:54.787
and all that--

00:01:54.787 --> 00:01:56.454
It doesn't-- matter.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.977
I swear to God it doesn't matter.

00:02:00.977 --> 00:02:03.088
If you back that up with the idea

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:05.648
that just playing those great songs

00:02:05.648 --> 00:02:07.625
in your great live band

00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:11.042
is enough reward for you then you're set.

00:02:12.541 --> 00:02:14.249
But you've got to be badass.

00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:16.916
You just have to be really good.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:19.205
It's the other things

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:23.306
that make up for your musical inability.

00:02:23.306 --> 00:02:25.973
As a drummer, I never felt like,

00:02:29.756 --> 00:02:32.237
I'm going to be on the cover
of Modern Drummer magazine

00:02:32.237 --> 00:02:34.328
because I'm the best drummer in the world.

00:02:34.328 --> 00:02:36.617
I just knew that if you put me on stage

00:02:36.617 --> 00:02:40.534
without a PA or floor
monitors in a small club,

00:02:42.485 --> 00:02:45.056
that I would beat the-- out of my drums

00:02:45.056 --> 00:02:46.272
so much that people would go,

00:02:46.272 --> 00:02:48.185
Goddamn, did you see that-- drummer?

00:02:48.185 --> 00:02:49.222
- Right.

00:02:49.222 --> 00:02:50.295
- Whoa.

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:53.342
And I walk away from every
show like, there you go.

00:02:53.342 --> 00:02:57.795
I just beat the piss out of
those things and people saw it.

00:02:57.795 --> 00:02:59.381
- People took notice for sure.

00:02:59.381 --> 00:03:00.799
- And that was great!

00:03:00.799 --> 00:03:03.302
So at the end of the show I
was as successful musician.

00:03:03.302 --> 00:03:06.453
Because I had achieved
what I wanted to achieve.

00:03:06.453 --> 00:03:08.040
So I honestly believe it.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.130
I know lots of musicians that went down to

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.394
South by Southwest and said
well that's easy for you to say.

00:03:13.394 --> 00:03:17.561
Man, I was in the same position
you are in 24 years ago.

00:03:19.003 --> 00:03:20.153
That was it.

00:03:20.153 --> 00:03:21.696
I worked at a furniture warehouse

00:03:21.696 --> 00:03:23.305
and I wanted people to like my music

00:03:23.305 --> 00:03:26.222
so I played out as much as I could.

00:03:27.059 --> 00:03:29.115
If you really, honestly, like I said,

00:03:29.115 --> 00:03:33.138
if you're passionate and driven
and focused in what you do,

00:03:33.138 --> 00:03:37.373
if you're really good at
it, people will take notice.

00:03:37.373 --> 00:03:39.305
That's basically it.

00:03:39.305 --> 00:03:41.621
I don't understand the industry.

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:45.050
I don't understand where music is headed.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.169
I don't really understand technology.

00:03:48.169 --> 00:03:52.403
I just know that when you walk into a club

00:03:52.403 --> 00:03:55.500
and you see a band that blows you away,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.601
you are going to follow that band.

00:03:57.601 --> 00:03:59.153
You're either going to buy their CD

00:03:59.153 --> 00:04:00.580
or you're going to find them online

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.984
or you're going to see them the
next time they come to play.

00:04:04.984 --> 00:04:07.323
That's what it takes.

00:04:07.323 --> 00:04:10.541
You don't have to stand in
line at the song contest on TV

00:04:10.541 --> 00:04:12.958
to become a popular musician.

00:04:14.073 --> 00:04:15.771
To stand in front of some judge

00:04:15.771 --> 00:04:17.389
that doesn't even play an instrument

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:19.054
on their own Goddamn record

00:04:19.054 --> 00:04:22.744
telling you you're not good enough-- that.

00:04:22.744 --> 00:04:25.577
Go blow people away in their face.

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.999
I honestly believe it's that simple.

00:04:32.999 --> 00:04:37.166
I directed music videos
before for The Foo Fighters.

00:04:38.162 --> 00:04:39.662
I mean, it's easy.

00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:44.653
I honestly believe that if you're focused

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.777
and passionate and driven,

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:54.847
you can achieve anything
you want to achieve in life.

00:04:54.847 --> 00:04:57.002
I honestly believe that.

00:04:57.002 --> 00:04:59.461
Because you'll figure it out.

00:04:59.461 --> 00:05:02.612
So I never took lessons to play the drums.

00:05:02.612 --> 00:05:04.569
I just figured it out.

00:05:04.569 --> 00:05:06.746
I never to lessons to play guitar.

00:05:06.746 --> 00:05:10.312
I took maybe a few, but I just
listened to Beatles records.

00:05:10.312 --> 00:05:12.562
I would listen and practice

00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:17.978
by myself in my bedroom obsessively.

00:05:17.978 --> 00:05:21.363
Throughout life, I've
always kind of figured

00:05:21.363 --> 00:05:23.307
that that's just how you do stuff.

00:05:23.307 --> 00:05:25.900
Like if you have a natural inclination

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.617
and a real drive to do something,

00:05:28.617 --> 00:05:30.791
that you can figure it out.

00:05:30.791 --> 00:05:32.528
- Was playing the guitar your real dream,

00:05:32.528 --> 00:05:34.549
your childhood dream?

00:05:34.549 --> 00:05:38.382
Kind of, because when I
was about 11 years old

00:05:39.657 --> 00:05:42.763
I was in I think seventh grade.

00:05:42.763 --> 00:05:44.941
From grade one to six,
you're in this one school.

00:05:44.941 --> 00:05:46.416
And then you go to this new school.

00:05:46.416 --> 00:05:49.781
I went to this new school
and there was this girl.

00:05:49.781 --> 00:05:51.691
This girl named Sandy.

00:05:51.691 --> 00:05:54.037
Oh she was so beautiful.

00:05:54.037 --> 00:05:58.431
She had big blue eyes and this
perfect heart shaped face.

00:05:58.431 --> 00:06:01.591
I was instantly in love with this chick.

00:06:01.591 --> 00:06:05.758
So the first day I asked her,
"Want to go steady with me?"

00:06:07.201 --> 00:06:09.369
She was like, "Okay"

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:11.818
So every day I'd meet her at her locker

00:06:11.818 --> 00:06:13.369
and we'd kiss at the locker

00:06:13.369 --> 00:06:15.371
and then she'd get on her
bus and I'd get on my bus

00:06:15.371 --> 00:06:18.031
and we'd go home and talk on the phone.

00:06:18.031 --> 00:06:22.198
After seven days, she said,
"You know, I'm new here

00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:28.734
"and I just don't want to get tied down."

00:06:30.792 --> 00:06:32.791
We were like 11 years old.

00:06:32.791 --> 00:06:34.524
(laughter)

00:06:34.524 --> 00:06:36.936
"I don't want to get tied
into a relationship."

00:06:36.936 --> 00:06:39.910
It absolutely broke my heart.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.410
I was heartbroken.

00:06:42.391 --> 00:06:45.396
And that night I had a dream

00:06:45.396 --> 00:06:47.889
that I was in an arena full of people

00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:50.111
and I was playing guitar.

00:06:50.111 --> 00:06:53.419
It's this packed arena and
they're all going, "Yeah!"

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:56.851
I'm playing and I look down
and she's staring at me

00:06:56.851 --> 00:06:58.895
and she's crying.

00:06:58.895 --> 00:07:01.895
(audience applause)

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.611
- Revenge (laughs).

00:07:07.611 --> 00:07:09.657
- My rock 'n roll revenge.

00:07:09.657 --> 00:07:11.790
You could have been my girlfriend.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.589
- We all have this dream

00:07:13.589 --> 00:07:15.567
but we just don't end up
like The Foo Fighters,

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:16.796
that's the problem.

00:07:16.796 --> 00:07:18.361
- Dreams come true.

00:07:18.361 --> 00:07:21.944
Honestly, making albums
to tape, it's easy.

00:07:23.343 --> 00:07:27.187
I think that people get scared because

00:07:27.187 --> 00:07:30.962
there's so much emphasis
put on perfection now

00:07:30.962 --> 00:07:32.757
that that's why people use computers.

00:07:32.757 --> 00:07:34.403
If you're a drummer and
you speed up a little bit

00:07:34.403 --> 00:07:35.708
or you slow down a little bit,

00:07:35.708 --> 00:07:39.520
you throw it in the computer
and make it sound perfect.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.555
Then if you sang a little
bit sharp or a little flat,

00:07:41.555 --> 00:07:43.442
you can smooth all of that out.

00:07:43.442 --> 00:07:44.950
And it just makes it sound--

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:49.163
you lose all the
personality and character.

00:07:49.163 --> 00:07:51.980
Imagine if you Pro Tooled a Who record.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:53.701
It wouldn't sound like The Who.

00:07:53.701 --> 00:07:56.015
It's like, wow that's tape.

00:07:56.015 --> 00:07:57.513
That's Monkey Wrench.

00:07:57.513 --> 00:07:58.461
- That's Monkey Wrench?

00:07:58.461 --> 00:07:59.591
That's incredible.

00:07:59.591 --> 00:08:01.341
- It's cool, you know?

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:07.321
With computers, they're
great for some things.

00:08:07.321 --> 00:08:10.187
But when it comes to making
a raw rock 'n roll record,

00:08:10.187 --> 00:08:12.305
you just can't beat it.

00:08:12.305 --> 00:08:14.552
You got to just go with tape.

00:08:14.552 --> 00:08:17.869
I stopped listening to
music after Kurt died

00:08:17.869 --> 00:08:19.993
because it was so
emotional just hearing it.

00:08:19.993 --> 00:08:20.989
I didn't want to hear it on the radio.

00:08:20.989 --> 00:08:21.870
I didn't want to play it.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.620
I didn't want to join another band.

00:08:25.749 --> 00:08:28.677
Then after awhile I realized
that it was the one thing

00:08:28.677 --> 00:08:31.681
that was going to help me
get through everything.

00:08:31.681 --> 00:08:33.882
So I started writing lyrics,
I started writing songs.

00:08:33.882 --> 00:08:36.410
And I went and recorded this demo tape.

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:38.660
("Big Me")

00:08:43.686 --> 00:08:45.283
A record company called and said,

00:08:45.283 --> 00:08:47.754
"Hey we want to release your record."

00:08:47.754 --> 00:08:50.757
And I said, "That's a
demo, that's not a band.

00:08:50.757 --> 00:08:53.341
"It's a thing I did by
myself in five days."

00:08:53.341 --> 00:08:54.436
- You played every instrument?

00:08:54.436 --> 00:08:56.848
- Yeah, so then I started to think,

00:08:56.848 --> 00:08:58.513
wow maybe I will try this.

00:08:58.513 --> 00:09:00.860
I've never been the singer of a band.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.093
- Was it a tough transition
from drummer to front man?

00:09:03.093 --> 00:09:05.843
- Absolutely, it took years, man.

00:09:06.917 --> 00:09:08.963
It look a long time.

00:09:08.963 --> 00:09:12.760
The most important thing is
that, whatever you're doing,

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.177
that it's a representation of your voice.

00:09:20.773 --> 00:09:23.164
Say you're using a computer

00:09:23.164 --> 00:09:24.584
or you're using an oboe

00:09:24.584 --> 00:09:26.491
or you're a drummer

00:09:26.491 --> 00:09:29.042
or a vocalist, whatever it is.

00:09:29.042 --> 00:09:33.075
The most important thing is
that it's really your voice.

00:09:33.075 --> 00:09:35.901
That it's coming from you.

00:09:35.901 --> 00:09:38.755
But it has to come from you.

00:09:38.755 --> 00:09:43.189
It's so easy just to
grab a loop and hit play

00:09:43.189 --> 00:09:45.236
and let it do its thing.

00:09:45.236 --> 00:09:48.106
- - that, that's somebody else's loop.

00:09:48.106 --> 00:09:50.711
That's somebody else's beat, man.

00:09:50.711 --> 00:09:52.239
Make your own beat.

00:09:52.239 --> 00:09:56.040
Take that guys beat,
step on it and-- it up

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.262
and then put it out
there, make it your own.

00:09:59.262 --> 00:10:00.262
You do that.

00:10:03.384 --> 00:10:07.551
I have always loved, whether
it's like Kraftwerk--

00:10:09.842 --> 00:10:12.301
I was one of the first
dorks in my neighborhood

00:10:12.301 --> 00:10:14.301
to get a Prodigy record.

00:10:15.295 --> 00:10:19.756
In like 1990 or whatever
year it was, or '91.

00:10:19.756 --> 00:10:21.866
I didn't have a pacifier and a glow stick.

00:10:21.866 --> 00:10:24.995
(audience laughter)

00:10:24.995 --> 00:10:26.745
But I liked the idea.

00:10:27.623 --> 00:10:29.589
Thank you very much.

00:10:29.589 --> 00:10:33.756
I liked the idea that these
people were doing something new.

00:10:36.741 --> 00:10:39.982
It didn't matter if it was as
a computer or a drum machine.

00:10:39.982 --> 00:10:44.147
It was something new, it was really cool.

00:10:44.147 --> 00:10:46.230
But lately, what is that?

00:10:47.589 --> 00:10:51.756
The last two days I had pool
parties for my kid's friends.

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:55.453
My wife turns on Pandora

00:10:55.453 --> 00:10:58.928
to the Blockbuster Hits station.

00:10:58.928 --> 00:11:00.928
It was like apocalyptic.

00:11:02.025 --> 00:11:03.358
I was terrified.

00:11:05.766 --> 00:11:08.806
I was like, the human race is going down

00:11:08.806 --> 00:11:10.639
such a dangerous path.

00:11:11.756 --> 00:11:14.070
We're all going to die.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:15.946
Everyone's worried about asteroids.

00:11:15.946 --> 00:11:18.196
- - that, turn on the radio.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:22.519
That will scare the-- out of you.

00:11:22.519 --> 00:11:26.352
I listen to that and I
just don't hear anyone.

00:11:29.536 --> 00:11:34.396
It's like two stupid people
talking to each other.

00:11:34.396 --> 00:11:36.845
There's just nothing there.

00:11:36.845 --> 00:11:38.845
It's devoid of anything.

00:11:41.426 --> 00:11:43.585
How could that be?

00:11:43.585 --> 00:11:45.583
It's terrible.

00:11:45.583 --> 00:11:48.511
(audience laughter)

00:11:48.511 --> 00:11:52.189
So the most important thing (laughs)

00:11:52.189 --> 00:11:54.165
I honestly think,

00:11:54.165 --> 00:11:55.931
is you have to be able to take

00:11:55.931 --> 00:11:59.348
whatever it is that someone else is using

00:12:00.189 --> 00:12:02.256
and make it your own.

00:12:02.256 --> 00:12:04.399
Or just do your own thing.

00:12:04.399 --> 00:12:06.759
I love being a drummer.

00:12:06.759 --> 00:12:10.056
It's such a nice position in a band.

00:12:10.056 --> 00:12:12.389
Everyone thinks you're dumb.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.924
What they don't realize is that

00:12:15.924 --> 00:12:18.717
if it weren't for you,
their band would suck.

00:12:18.717 --> 00:12:21.422
(laughs)

00:12:21.422 --> 00:12:22.862
I've always loved being the drummer.

00:12:22.862 --> 00:12:27.791
I don't necessarily need
to be the dude out front.

00:12:27.791 --> 00:12:31.532
I never imagined myself a front man.

00:12:31.532 --> 00:12:34.749
At the same time, I don't
mind bringing the party

00:12:34.749 --> 00:12:36.964
to 85,000 people at the lip of the stage

00:12:36.964 --> 00:12:39.631
being the front man of the band.

00:12:41.087 --> 00:12:43.076
It's nice to have both.

00:12:43.076 --> 00:12:45.358
There's that balance between the two.

00:12:45.358 --> 00:12:47.356
If you ever start feeling like

00:12:47.356 --> 00:12:49.055
nobody's paying attention to me.

00:12:49.055 --> 00:12:53.211
When then you go and sing in
The Foo Fighters for awhile.

00:12:53.211 --> 00:12:54.141
Then when you're like,

00:12:54.141 --> 00:12:55.951
Goddamn I'm sick of all this--

00:12:55.951 --> 00:12:58.687
go back to being the drummer
in The Vultures or whatever.

00:12:58.687 --> 00:13:02.463
It's weird, as a musician,
you want to be great.

00:13:02.463 --> 00:13:04.074
You want to be good.

00:13:04.074 --> 00:13:08.042
And you sort of set these funny
expectations for yourself.

00:13:08.042 --> 00:13:11.302
One of the funniest things
that Fogarty ever said to me,

00:13:11.302 --> 00:13:15.887
He was like, "Yeah, I was
learning to play the drums

00:13:15.887 --> 00:13:18.154
"but I realized that I
would never be as good

00:13:18.154 --> 00:13:22.659
"as John Bonham from Led
Zeppelin, so I stopped."

00:13:22.659 --> 00:13:25.242
Who is as good as John? Nobody.

00:13:26.199 --> 00:13:28.144
Like, what the-- how could you do that?

00:13:28.144 --> 00:13:31.755
There should be no right or wrong.

00:13:31.755 --> 00:13:35.922
You should just be cool with
what you do and how you sound.

00:13:36.895 --> 00:13:40.391
It's one of the things that
I have a problem with a lot

00:13:40.391 --> 00:13:43.788
of those song contest shows on TV.

00:13:43.788 --> 00:13:47.980
Not only because it's
corporate cheese or whatever.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:52.147
But I have a problem with, like
if my kid walked up on stage

00:13:53.041 --> 00:13:56.875
and sang her heart out
as best as she could,

00:13:56.875 --> 00:14:01.042
and Jennifer Lopez said, "No,
sorry that's not good enough."

00:14:04.251 --> 00:14:06.001
I would be so pissed.

00:14:07.657 --> 00:14:10.324
Because that's not how it works.

00:14:11.357 --> 00:14:15.524
You should go to a yard
sale and buy an old guitar.

00:14:16.833 --> 00:14:21.281
Then go find a friend and
get them in your garage

00:14:21.281 --> 00:14:23.948
and then just suck and be awful.

00:14:25.287 --> 00:14:27.002
And then write some songs

00:14:27.002 --> 00:14:30.600
and become the biggest band in the world.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.336
(audience cheers)

00:14:33.336 --> 00:14:35.669
Because that's how it works.

00:14:36.821 --> 00:14:39.996
It works other ways too, I'm sure.

00:14:39.996 --> 00:14:42.829
As a young musician, first of all,

00:14:44.032 --> 00:14:47.684
you're not going to
make a living doing it.

00:14:47.684 --> 00:14:49.875
Accept that, realize that.

00:14:49.875 --> 00:14:53.486
Because then if you do, you're stoked.

00:14:53.486 --> 00:14:56.512
The reward of playing music,

00:14:56.512 --> 00:14:59.058
playing music should be the
reward of playing music.

00:14:59.058 --> 00:15:00.725
That's what you get.

00:15:02.323 --> 00:15:04.102
When I was a little kid,

00:15:04.102 --> 00:15:05.331
or when I was playing

00:15:05.331 --> 00:15:06.960
punk rock music as a teenager,

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.616
there was never any career opportunity

00:15:08.616 --> 00:15:11.407
because we played punk rock music.

00:15:11.407 --> 00:15:13.108
Nobody wanted to buy that.

00:15:13.108 --> 00:15:14.179
But we loved to do it.

00:15:14.179 --> 00:15:15.762
It was so much fun.

00:15:17.154 --> 00:15:19.442
If you start from a place like that.

00:15:19.442 --> 00:15:20.717
That you just love to create

00:15:20.717 --> 00:15:22.147
and you just love to play music

00:15:22.147 --> 00:15:23.555
and you can't stop thinking about it

00:15:23.555 --> 00:15:25.611
and you do it every day

00:15:25.611 --> 00:15:26.929
and you do it by yourself

00:15:26.929 --> 00:15:28.606
or you do it with your friends.

00:15:28.606 --> 00:15:29.900
And that's enough for you

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:33.456
to survive the rest of
your life, that's great.

00:15:33.456 --> 00:15:35.668
Then once you start getting good,

00:15:35.668 --> 00:15:37.254
you start playing for people.

00:15:37.254 --> 00:15:38.941
And once you start playing for people

00:15:38.941 --> 00:15:41.725
if you're good, then those
people will tell other people,

00:15:41.725 --> 00:15:43.692
"That band's really good"

00:15:43.692 --> 00:15:45.179
So then all of a sudden you're playing

00:15:45.179 --> 00:15:47.839
to all these different people.

00:15:47.839 --> 00:15:50.821
That to me is the most important thing.

00:15:50.821 --> 00:15:52.876
When it's you on stage.

00:15:52.876 --> 00:15:56.341
When people see another person doing that,

00:15:56.341 --> 00:15:58.678
it's really inspiring.

00:15:58.678 --> 00:16:03.437
It's cool just to see a human
being jamming, that's great.

00:16:03.437 --> 00:16:05.354
Guilt, guilt is cancer.

00:16:08.019 --> 00:16:10.099
It will confine you.

00:16:10.099 --> 00:16:11.099
Torture you.

00:16:12.488 --> 00:16:15.271
Destroy you as an artist.

00:16:15.271 --> 00:16:18.636
It's a wall, it's a
black hole, it's a thief.

00:16:18.636 --> 00:16:21.238
It will keep you from you.

00:16:21.238 --> 00:16:23.285
Remember learning your first song or riff

00:16:23.285 --> 00:16:25.810
or writing your first lyric?

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:27.810
There was no guilt then.

00:16:29.453 --> 00:16:32.850
Remember when there was no right or wrong?

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:36.816
Remember that simple reward
of just playing music?

00:16:36.816 --> 00:16:39.466
You are still and will always be

00:16:39.466 --> 00:16:41.549
that person at your core.

00:16:42.393 --> 00:16:43.476
The musician.

00:16:44.785 --> 00:16:47.713
And the musician comes first.

00:16:47.713 --> 00:16:49.296
- - guilty pleasure.

00:16:50.193 --> 00:16:52.276
How about, just pleasure?

00:16:54.127 --> 00:16:57.977
I can truthfully say
aloud that Gangnam Style

00:16:57.977 --> 00:17:01.779
is one of my favorite
songs of the past decade.

00:17:01.779 --> 00:17:04.988
(audience laughter)

00:17:04.988 --> 00:17:07.847
(audience applause)

00:17:07.847 --> 00:17:08.680
It is.

00:17:10.049 --> 00:17:11.492
Is it any better or worse than

00:17:11.492 --> 00:17:14.285
the latest Atoms for Peace album?

00:17:14.285 --> 00:17:15.118
Hmm.

00:17:16.877 --> 00:17:19.234
If only we had a celebrity panel of judges

00:17:19.234 --> 00:17:21.317
to determine that for us.

00:17:22.564 --> 00:17:24.147
What would J Lo do?

00:17:25.984 --> 00:17:28.252
Paging Pitchfork, come
in, come in, Pitchfork,

00:17:28.252 --> 00:17:30.119
we need you to help us determine

00:17:30.119 --> 00:17:31.786
the value of a song.

00:17:34.399 --> 00:17:35.399
Who-- cares.

00:17:37.976 --> 00:17:40.976
(audience applause)

00:17:48.579 --> 00:17:51.452
Who is to say what's a good voice

00:17:51.452 --> 00:17:53.664
and what's not a good voice?

00:17:53.664 --> 00:17:54.497
The Voice?

00:17:55.418 --> 00:17:58.770
(audience laughter)

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.647
Imagine Bob Dylan standing there

00:18:00.647 --> 00:18:02.346
singing Blowing in the Wind

00:18:02.346 --> 00:18:05.242
in front of Christina Aguilera.

00:18:05.242 --> 00:18:08.242
(audience laughter)

00:18:10.436 --> 00:18:14.638
Mmm, I think you sound a
little nasally and sharp.

00:18:14.638 --> 00:18:17.638
(audience laughter)

00:18:18.504 --> 00:18:19.837
It's your voice.

00:18:22.326 --> 00:18:25.243
Cherish it, respect it, nurture it.

00:18:26.541 --> 00:18:30.708
Challenge it, stretch it,
scream it until it's gone.

00:18:33.022 --> 00:18:37.501
Because everyone's blessed
with at least that.

00:18:37.501 --> 00:18:40.501
And who knows how long it will last.

00:18:42.664 --> 00:18:45.831
- Whether he's playing protective dad,

00:18:48.755 --> 00:18:49.755
or Rock God,

00:18:54.229 --> 00:18:58.306
Dave Grohl is exactly
where he wants to be.

